# WordPress, Tailwind, and theme.json

Apply this rule only when the project uses WordPress together with
Tailwind and `theme.json`. That includes Radicle/Sage themes and other
WordPress themes on this combination.

Do not apply it to Laravel, React, Rails, or other non-WordPress
stacks. Do not apply it to WordPress projects that do not use both
Tailwind and `theme.json`.

Current-First Engineering in `00-platform` still applies. Inspect the
installed WordPress and Tailwind versions and the CSS this project
actually generates. Handbook examples go stale: WordPress has emitted
both bare element selectors and `:root :where(...)` wrappers. Follow
the generated stylesheet for the installed version.

## Source of truth

Project Tailwind tokens are the typography and color source of truth.
On Tailwind v4 that is the CSS-first theme (`@theme` and the version's
other theme APIs). On Tailwind v3 that is the v3 theme configuration.
Do not upgrade Tailwind to satisfy this rule.

Generate or align `theme.json` presets with those tokens so editors
get the approved fonts, sizes, and colors. Point palette, font-family,
and font-size presets at the CSS variables the Tailwind theme already
defines. WordPress preset variables (`--wp--preset--color--*`,
`--wp--preset--font-size--*`, and font-family presets) may alias those
tokens. Do not keep a second, independent set of color or type values
in `theme.json`.

Use `theme.json` for WordPress settings and for intentional content
defaults. Keep the file. Do not remove `theme.json` to escape a
cascade conflict.

## Editorial typography

WordPress generates CSS from `theme.json` for both the block editor
and the public site. Top-level `styles.elements` for `heading` and
`h1`-`h6` emit heading element selectors. Some blocks, including
`core/paragraph`, can emit an element selector such as `p`. Treat
lists the same way: a generated `ul` or `ol` rule with no content
scope is unscoped element typography.

Avoid that unscoped element typography. It can override Tailwind
utilities on custom page components. Tailwind v4 keeps utilities in
`@layer utilities`. Unlayered WordPress CSS beats layered utilities
even when the utility is more specific. Tailwind v3 utilities are
unlayered unless the project opted into layers. Read the actual
stylesheets for this project.

Scope editorial typography to both of these surfaces, and to no wider
target:

- the block editor content canvas
- the published content region that wraps block content on the public
  site

Custom templates and components keep control of their typography
through the project design system and Tailwind utilities. A descendant
element selector on a broad wrapper still matches component headings
rendered inside it, including every `h1` under `.editor-styles-wrapper`
or under the content region. Prefer selectors that match editorial
blocks. Keep component roots outside those selectors.

A core heading block style that targets `.wp-block-heading` is a
block-scoped default. It applies in the editor and on the published
site for that block. Use a paragraph or list block style only when
its generated selector is block-scoped. If WordPress emits `p`, `ul`,
or `ol` for that block, put the default in scoped theme CSS so it
does not match custom components.

Custom CSS that ships through `theme.json` or the theme stylesheet is
global until its selectors are limited to the canvas and the published
content region.

Editor and published block content must share the same tokens and the
same scoping decision. Confirm the installed editor canvas (iframed or
not) and the theme's real content wrapper. Keep the Tailwind token
scale as the only type scale.

If headings are responsive, align the preset or the scoped editorial
rule with the token. A token that is already fluid can be referenced
directly. WordPress `@mobile` and `@tablet` keys, and
`settings.typography.fluid`, are available when they match that token.
Do not add a second clamp or a second breakpoint map that diverges
from the Tailwind theme.

Do not add `!important` to utilities or to `theme.json` styles to win
this cascade.

## Site-wide element rules

A site-wide element rule is appropriate only when it is a document
baseline that custom components are expected to inherit, and those
components do not override it with utilities. Typical cases are `body`
font-family or a single root text color.

Heading scale, paragraph measure, and list styling belong in the
scoped editorial styles above when custom components set them with
Tailwind utilities.

Before keeping a site-wide element rule:

1. Read the generated selector, not only the `theme.json` source.
2. Check specificity and whether the rule is unlayered.
3. Check it against Tailwind's cascade layers. Unlayered CSS wins
   over `@layer utilities` regardless of specificity.
4. Drop the rule if it overrides component utilities. Do not repair
   that with `!important`.

## Verification

When changing `theme.json`, editorial typography, or component type
that shares this cascade, check all of the following:

- Editor: a heading, paragraph, and list in the content canvas use
  the approved fonts, sizes, and colors.
- Published block content: those same blocks match the editor.
- Custom page components: Tailwind utilities control their headings,
  paragraphs, and lists. Scaffold element CSS does not override them.
- Generated CSS: the global stylesheet has no unscoped `h1`-`h6`, `p`,
  `ul`, or `ol` typography from `theme.json`, unless a site-wide
  exception above was checked against cascade layers.
- Responsive headings: at a narrow and a wide viewport, editor
  headings, published block headings, and custom-component headings
  each follow the scale the design system defines for that surface.
